course: dictionary
lesson: 1893
title: A rank, a name, and a tricky spelling
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A colonel is a high-ranking officer in the army, air force or marines. A colonel is above a major and below a general. In history and stories, colonels lead large groups of soldiers, so the rank is a big job.
explain: Look at the capital letter. «Mia's grandma in Georgia has a cockerel called Colonel who chases anyone in shorts.» A cockerel cannot be an officer, so here Colonel is a name. Swap in your meaning: "a cockerel called high-ranking officer" makes no sense, so you know this is a name.
explain: Spelling can fool you. Colonel is said "kernel", not "co-lo-nel", because it came from French "coronel", with an r. The spelling was later changed to look like Italian "colonnello". When a word is hard to say, say it slowly and check a dictionary.
fact: A colonel is a high-ranking officer in the army, air force or marines.
fact: A colonel is above a major and below a general.
fact: Colonel is said "kernel", even though it is spelled with an "lo" in the middle.
fact: Colonel Sanders is the face of a fried chicken brand, and Colonel Mustard is a character in the game Cluedo.
fact: A capital C can show that Colonel is a name, as in «Mia learns to carry a bucket between her and Colonel and walk calmly.»
exercise: In «Mia's grandma in Georgia has a cockerel called Colonel who chases anyone in shorts», what is Colonel? | A bird's name | A high-ranking officer in the army | *A cockerel's name | A kind of fried chicken
exercise: What is a colonel? | A soldier who cooks for the army | A rope used by sailors | *A high-ranking officer in the army, air force or marines | A type of fried chicken
exercise: How is colonel said? | *Like "kernel" | Co-lo-nel, all three parts said clearly | Like "colon" with a long o | Like "cornel" with a hard c
test: In «Mia learns to carry a bucket between her and Colonel and walk calmly», is Colonel a rank or a name? | *A name, because it is the cockerel | A name, because it is in Georgia | A rank, because it has a capital letter | A rank, because it sounds like a soldier
test: Where does the word colonel come from? | From the game Cluedo | From Georgia | From Italian "colonnello", and never changed | *From French "coronel", with an r
exam: Where does a colonel sit in the ranks? | Below a major and above a private | Below a private and above a sergeant | *Above a major and below a general | Above a general and below the king
